<handout>
  <title>Practice Exam 4</title>

<section>
<title>Topics</title>

    <p>
      Sections 5.1, 5.2, 5.3, 5.4, 5.5, 5.6
    </p>

    <p>
      Be sure to try each question before looking at the solutions.
    </p>

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-practiceexam4">
    <title>Questions</title>

 <exercise xml:id="intro-systems-exam">
  <!-- <title>Rewriting a Higher-Order ODE as a First-Order System</title> -->

  <statement>
    <p>
      Consider the second-order differential equation
    </p>

    <me>
      y'' - 3y' + 2y = 0.
    </me>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Introduce new variables to rewrite this equation as an equivalent
          first-order system in two variables.
        </li>

        <li>
          Express the resulting system using vector notation
          <m>\vec{x}' = A\vec{x}</m> for some matrix <m>A</m>.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Let <m>x_1 = y</m> and <m>x_2 = y'</m>.  
      Then compute <m>x_1'</m> and <m>x_2'</m> using the original equation.
      Remember that <m>x_1' = y'</m> and that <m>y''</m> can be replaced with an
      expression involving <m>x_1</m> and <m>x_2</m>.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      One possible system is
      </p>
      <me>
      \begin{aligned}
      x_1' \amp= x_2, \\
      x_2' \amp= -2x_1 + 3x_2.
      \end{aligned}
      </me>

      <p>
      In vector form,
      </p>

      <me>
      \vec{x}' =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      0 \amp 1 \\
      -2 \amp 3
      \end{bmatrix}
      \vec{x}.
      </me>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      (a)  
      We introduce the standard substitution for converting a second-order ODE
      into a first-order system:
    </p>

    <me>
      x_1 = y, \qquad x_2 = y'.
    </me>

    <p>
      Then
    </p>

    <me>
      x_1' = y' = x_2.
    </me>

    <p>
      To compute <m>x_2'</m>, use the original equation:
    </p>

    <me>
      y'' - 3y' + 2y = 0
      \quad\Rightarrow\quad
      y'' = 3y' - 2y.
    </me>

    <p>
      Substituting <m>x_1 = y</m> and <m>x_2 = y'</m> gives
    </p>

    <me>
      x_2' = 3x_2 - 2x_1.
    </me>

    <p>
      Therefore the first-order system is
    </p>

    <me>
      \begin{aligned}
      x_1' \amp= x_2, \\
      x_2' \amp= -2x_1 + 3x_2.
      \end{aligned}
    </me>

    <p>
      (b)
      In vector–matrix notation, with
    </p>

    <me>
      \vec{x} =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      x_1 \\
      x_2
      \end{bmatrix},
    </me>

    <p>
      the system becomes
    </p>

    <me>
      \vec{x}' =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      0 \amp 1 \\
      -2 \amp 3
      \end{bmatrix}
      \vec{x}.
    </me>

    <p>
      This is the standard first-order vector representation of the original
      second-order equation.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="two-tank-mixing-system">
  <!-- <title>Setting Up a System for a Two-Tank Mixing Problem</title> -->

  <statement>
    <p>
      Two tanks are connected in such a way that fluid flows between them.
      Tank A contains 200 liters of brine, and Tank B contains 150 liters.
      At time <m>t=0</m>, Tank A contains 30 kg of salt and Tank B contains 5 kg.
    </p>

    <p>
      A pump moves well‑stirred brine from Tank A into Tank B at a rate of
      <m>5 \text{ L/min}</m>.  
      Another pump moves well‑stirred brine from Tank B back into Tank A at a rate of
      <m>3 \text{ L/min}</m>.  
      There are no other inflows or outflows, so volumes remain constant.
    </p>

    <p>
      Let <m>x(t)</m> be the amount of salt (in kg) in Tank A at time <m>t</m>,
      and let <m>y(t)</m> be the amount of salt in Tank B at time <m>t</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Task:</strong>  
      Derive a system of differential equations for <m>x(t)</m> and <m>y(t)</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
    <figure xml:id="two-tank-diagram">
      <caption>
        Two interconnected mixing tanks with flow rates.
      </caption>

      <image width="80%">
       <description>
        Diagram showing two tanks, Tank A on the left and Tank B on the right
        with arrows indicating 5 liters per minute from A to B and
        3 liters per minute from B to A.
      </description>
        <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0]

% Tank A
\draw[thick] (0,0) rectangle (3,2);
\node at (1.5,2.3) {Tank A};
\node at (1.5,1) {200 L};

% Tank B
\draw[thick] (6,0) rectangle (9,2);
\node at (7.5,2.3) {Tank B};
\node at (7.5,1) {150 L};

% Arrow A -> B (5 L/min)
\draw[->, thick] (3,1.5) -- (6,1.5);
\node at (4.5,1.8) {5 L/min};

% Arrow B -> A (3 L/min)
\draw[->, thick] (6,0.5) -- (3,0.5);
\node at (4.5,0.2) {3 L/min};

\end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>
</p>

  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Use the standard “rate in minus rate out” principle.
      The concentration in each tank is “amount of salt divided by volume.”
      For example, the concentration in Tank A is <m>x(t)/200</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Multiply each concentration by its respective flow rate to obtain
      salt transport rates.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      The system is
    </p>

    <me>
      \begin{aligned}
      x' \amp= \frac{3}{150}y - \frac{5}{200}x, \\
      y' \amp= \frac{5}{200}x - \frac{3}{150}y.
      \end{aligned}
    </me>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      The concentration in Tank A is <m>x/200</m> (kg/L),
      and Tank B has concentration <m>y/150</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Tank A:</strong>  
      Salt flows <em>in</em> from Tank B at rate
    </p>

    <me>
      (\text{flow rate from B to A}) \cdot (\text{concentration in B})
      = 3 \cdot \frac{y}{150}.
    </me>

    <p>
      Salt flows <em>out</em> to Tank B at rate
    </p>

    <me>
      5 \cdot \frac{x}{200}.
    </me>

    <p>
      So
    </p>

    <me>
      x' = 3\frac{y}{150} - 5\frac{x}{200}.
    </me>

    <p>
      <strong>Tank B:</strong>  
      Salt flows <em>in</em> from Tank A at
    </p>

    <me>
      5 \cdot \frac{x}{200},
    </me>

    <p>
      and flows <em>out</em> to Tank A at
    </p>

    <me>
      3 \cdot \frac{y}{150}.
    </me>

    <p>
      Therefore
    </p>

    <me>
      y' = 5\frac{x}{200} - 3\frac{y}{150}.
    </me>

    <p>
      This yields the system:
    </p>

    <me>
      \begin{aligned}
      x' \amp= \frac{3}{150}y - \frac{5}{200}x, \\
      y' \amp= \frac{5}{200}x - \frac{3}{150}y,
      \end{aligned}
    </me>

    <p>
      which correctly models the exchange of salt between the two tanks.
    </p>
  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="matrix-linear-system-exam">
  <!-- <title>Matrix Form and Matrix–Vector Computation</title> -->

  <statement>
    <p>
      Consider the system of differential equations
    </p>

    <me>
      \begin{aligned}
      x' \amp= 4x - y, \\
      y' \amp= 2x + 3y.
      \end{aligned}
    </me>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Write this system in matrix–vector form <m>\vec{x}' = A\vec{x}</m>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Compute <m>A\vec{v}</m> for the vector  
          <m>\vec{v} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -2 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      To write the system in matrix form, place the coefficients of <m>x</m> and 
      <m>y</m> into a matrix so that
    </p>

    <me>
      A = 
      \begin{bmatrix}
      4 \amp -1 \\
      2 \amp 3
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>

    <p>
      For part (b), recall the matrix–vector multiplication rule:
    </p>

    <me>
      A
      \begin{bmatrix} v_1 \\ v_2 \end{bmatrix}
      =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      4v_1 - v_2 \\
      2v_1 + 3v_2
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      <m>
      A =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      4 \amp -1 \\
      2 \amp 3
      \end{bmatrix},
      \qquad
      A\vec{v} =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      6 \\
      -4
      \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      (a)  
      The system
    </p>

    <me>
      x' = 4x - y, \qquad y' = 2x + 3y
    </me>

    <p>
      can be written in vector form as
    </p>

    <me>
      \vec{x}' =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      4 \amp -1 \\
      2 \amp 3
      \end{bmatrix}
      \vec{x},
    </me>

    <p>
      where
    </p>

    <me>
      \vec{x} =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      x \\
      y
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>

    <p>
      (b)  
      To compute <m>A\vec{v}</m> with  
      <m>\vec{v}=\begin{bmatrix}1 \\ -2\end{bmatrix}</m>, perform the matrix–vector product:
    </p>

    <me>
      A\vec{v}
      =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      4 \amp -1 \\
      2 \amp 3
      \end{bmatrix}
      \begin{bmatrix}
      1 \\
      -2
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>

    <p>
      Compute each entry:
    </p>

    <me>
      \begin{aligned}
      4(1) - (-2) \amp= 4 + 2 = 6, \\
      2(1) + 3(-2) \amp= 2 - 6 = -4.
      \end{aligned}
    </me>

    <p>
      Therefore,
    </p>

    <me>
      A\vec{v}
      =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      6 \\
      -4
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>
  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="linsys-diagonal-example">
  <!-- <title>Solving a Linear System with a Diagonal Matrix</title> -->

  <statement>
    <p>
      Consider the linear system of differential equations
    </p>

    <me>
      \vec{x}' = A\vec{x},
      \qquad
      A =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      -2 \amp 0 \\
      0 \amp 5
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>

    <p>
      with initial condition
    </p>

    <me>
      \vec{x}(0) =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      4 \\
      -3
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>Write the system in scalar form.</li>
        <li>Solve the system for <m>\vec{x}(t)</m>.</li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Because <m>A</m> is diagonal, the system decouples:
    </p>

    <me>
      x_1' = -2x_1,
      \qquad
      x_2' = 5x_2.
    </me>

    <p>
      Solve each equation separately.  
      Recall that the general solution of <m>x' = kx</m> is
    </p>

    <me>
      x(t) = C e^{kt}.
    </me>

    <p>
      Then use the initial condition to determine the constants.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      The solution is
    </p>

    <me>
      \vec{x}(t) =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      4e^{-2t} \\
      -3e^{5t}
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      (a)  Since <m>A</m> is diagonal, the system becomes
    </p>

    <me>
      \begin{aligned}
      x_1' \amp= -2x_1, \\
      x_2' \amp= 5x_2.
      \end{aligned}
    </me>

    <p>
      (b)  
      Solve each equation separately.
    </p>

    <p>
      For <m>x_1' = -2x_1</m>:
    </p>

    <me>
      x_1(t) = C_1 e^{-2t}.
    </me>

    <p>
      For <m>x_2' = 5x_2</m>:
    </p>

    <me>
      x_2(t) = C_2 e^{5t}.
    </me>

    <p>
      Apply the initial condition
      <m>\vec{x}(0) = \begin{bmatrix}4 \\ -3\end{bmatrix}</m>.
    </p>

    <me>
      x_1(0) = C_1 = 4,
      \qquad
      x_2(0) = C_2 = -3.
    </me>

    <p>
      Thus the solution is
    </p>

    <me>
      \vec{x}(t) =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      4e^{-2t} \\
      -3e^{5t}
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>

    <p>
      The system is completely decoupled, so each component evolves independently
      according to its exponential growth or decay rate.
    </p>
  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="eigenmethod-linear-system">
  <!-- <title>Solving a Linear System Using the Eigenvalue Method</title> -->

  <statement>
    <p>
      Consider the system of differential equations
    </p>

    <me>
      \vec{x}' =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      5 \amp -2 \\
      4 \amp -1
      \end{bmatrix}
      \vec{x},
      \qquad
      \vec{x}(0) =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      3 \\
      1
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>Find the eigenvalues of the matrix.</li>
        <li>Find an eigenvector for each eigenvalue.</li>
        <li>Write the general solution of the system.</li>
        <li>Use the initial condition to find the specific solution <m>\vec{x}(t)</m>.</li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Begin by computing the characteristic polynomial:
    </p>

    <me>
      \det(A - \lambda I)
      = 
      \begin{vmatrix}
      5-\lambda \amp -2 \\
      4 \amp -1-\lambda
      \end{vmatrix}.
    </me>

    <p>
      After finding the eigenvalues, solve <m>(A - \lambda I)\vec{v} = \vec{0}</m>  
      to find eigenvectors.  
      Each eigenpair gives a solution of the form <m>e^{\lambda t}\vec{v}</m>.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      Eigenvalues: <m>\lambda_1 = 3</m>, <m>\lambda_2 = 1</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Corresponding eigenvectors:
    </p>

    <me>
      \vec{v}_1 =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      1 \\
      1
      \end{bmatrix},
      \qquad
      \vec{v}_2 =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      1 \\
      2
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>

    <p>
      Specific solution:
    </p>

    <me>
      \vec{x}(t)
      =
      2e^{3t}
      \begin{bmatrix}
      1 \\
      1
      \end{bmatrix}
      +
      1e^{t}
      \begin{bmatrix}
      1 \\
      2
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      (a)  Compute the characteristic polynomial:
    </p>

    <me>
      \det(A - \lambda I)
      =
      (5-\lambda)(-1-\lambda) - (-2)(4).
    </me>

    <me>
      = (5-\lambda)(-1-\lambda) + 8
      = ( -5 -5\lambda + \lambda + \lambda^2 ) + 8
      = \lambda^2 -4\lambda + 3.
    </me>

    <p>
      Solve <m>\lambda^2 - 4\lambda + 3 = 0</m>:
    </p>

    <me>
      \lambda = 1, \; 3.
    </me>

    <p>
      (b)  Find eigenvectors.
    </p>

    <p>
      For <m>\lambda_1 = 3</m>, solve <m>(A - 3I)\vec{v} = 0</m>:
    </p>

    <me>
      A - 3I =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      2 \amp -2 \\
      4 \amp -4
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>

    <p>
      The rows are multiples; a solution is
    </p>

    <me>
      \vec{v}_1 =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      1 \\
      1
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>

    <p>
      For <m>\lambda_2 = 1</m>, solve <m>(A - I)\vec{v} = 0</m>:
    </p>

    <me>
      A - I =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      4 \amp -2 \\
      4 \amp -2
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>

    <p>
      A solution is
    </p>

    <me>
      \vec{v}_2 =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      1 \\
      2
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>

    <p>
      (c)  The general solution is
    </p>

    <me>
      \vec{x}(t)
      =
      C_1 e^{3t}
      \begin{bmatrix}
      1 \\
      1
      \end{bmatrix}
      +
      C_2 e^{t}
      \begin{bmatrix}
      1 \\
      2
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>

    <p>
      (d)  Apply <m>\vec{x}(0) = \begin{bmatrix}3 \\ 1\end{bmatrix}</m>:
    </p>

    <me>
      C_1
      \begin{bmatrix}
      1 \\
      1
      \end{bmatrix}
      +
      C_2
      \begin{bmatrix}
      1 \\
      2
      \end{bmatrix}
      =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      3 \\
      1
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>

    <p>
      Solve the system:
    </p>

    <me>
      \begin{aligned}
      C_1 + C_2 \amp= 3, \\
      C_1 + 2C_2 \amp= 1.
      \end{aligned}
    </me>

    <p>
      Subtract the first equation from the second:
    </p>

    <me>
      C_2 = -2,
      \qquad
      C_1 = 5.
    </me>

    <p>
      Thus the specific solution is
    </p>

    <me>
      \vec{x}(t)
      =
      5e^{3t}
      \begin{bmatrix}
      1 \\
      1
      \end{bmatrix}
      +
      (-2)e^{t}
      \begin{bmatrix}
      1 \\
      2
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>

    <p>
      Simplifying gives
    </p>

    <me>
      \vec{x}(t)
      =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      5e^{3t} - 2e^{t} \\
      5e^{3t} - 4e^{t}
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>
  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="eigenmethod-complex-clean-corrected">
  <!-- <title>Linear System with Complex Eigenvalues</title> -->

  <statement>
    <p>
      Consider the system
    </p>

    <me>
      \vec{x}' =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      3 \amp -4 \\
      1 \amp  3
      \end{bmatrix}
      \vec{x},
      \qquad
      \vec{x}(0) =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      1 \\
      2
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>Find the eigenvalues of the matrix.</li>
        <li>Find an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue with positive imaginary part.</li>
        <li>Write the real general solution of the system.</li>
        <li>Use the initial condition to find the specific solution <m>\vec{x}(t)</m>.</li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      The characteristic polynomial is
    </p>

    <me>
      (3-\lambda)^2 + 4.
    </me>

    <p>
      For <m>\lambda = a + bi</m> and eigenvector <m>\vec{v} = \vec{p} + i\vec{q}</m>, 
      the real solution is built from
    </p>

    <me>
      e^{at}\big(\vec{p}\cos(bt) - \vec{q}\sin(bt)\big)
      \qquad\text{and}\qquad
      e^{at}\big(\vec{p}\sin(bt) + \vec{q}\cos(bt)\big).
    </me>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      Eigenvalues: <m>\lambda = 3 \pm 2i</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      An eigenvector for <m>3 + 2i</m> is
    </p>

    <me>
      \vec{v}
      =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      2i \\
      1
      \end{bmatrix}
      =
      \vec{p} + i\vec{q},
      \qquad
      \vec{p} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix},
      \quad
      \vec{q} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>

    <p>
      The specific real solution is
    </p>

       <me>
      \vec{x}(t)
      =
      e^{3t}
      \begin{bmatrix}
      \cos(2t)-4\sin(2t) \\
      2\cos(2t)+\frac{1}{2}\sin(2t)
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>(a) Compute eigenvalues.</p>

    <me>
      \det(A - \lambda I)
      =
      (3-\lambda)^2 - (-4)(1)
      =
      (3-\lambda)^2 + 4.
    </me>

    <me>
      (3-\lambda)^2 + 4
      =
      \lambda^2 - 6\lambda + 13.
    </me>

    <me>
      \lambda
      =
      \frac{6 \pm \sqrt{36 - 52}}{2}
      =
      3 \pm 2i.
    </me>

    <p>(b) Eigenvector for <m>\lambda = 3 + 2i</m>.</p>

    <me>
      A - (3+2i)I
      =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      -2i \amp -4 \\
      1 \amp -2i
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>

    <p>
      First row:
    </p>

    <me>
      -2i\,v_1 - 4v_2 = 0
      \quad\Rightarrow\quad
      v_1 = 2i\,v_2.
    </me>

    <p>
      Choose <m>v_2=1</m>.  
      Then <m>v_1 = 2i</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      So an eigenvector is
    </p>

    <me>
      \vec{v}
      =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      2i \\
      1
      \end{bmatrix}
      =
      \vec{p} + i\vec{q},
      \qquad
      \vec{p}=\begin{bmatrix}0 \\ 1\end{bmatrix},
      \quad
      \vec{q}=\begin{bmatrix}2 \\ 0\end{bmatrix}.
    </me>

    <p>(c) General real solution.</p>

    <me>
      \vec{x}(t)
      =
      e^{3t}
      \Big(
      C_1(\vec{p}\cos(2t) - \vec{q}\sin(2t))
      +
      C_2(\vec{p}\sin(2t) + \vec{q}\cos(2t))
      \Big).
    </me>

    <p>
      Substitute <m>\vec{p}</m> and <m>\vec{q}</m>:
    </p>

    <me>
      \vec{x}(t)
      =
      e^{3t}
      \left[
      C_1
      \begin{bmatrix}
      -2\sin(2t) \\
      \cos(2t)
      \end{bmatrix}
      +
      C_2
      \begin{bmatrix}
      2\cos(2t) \\
      \sin(2t)
      \end{bmatrix}
      \right].
    </me>

    <p>(d) Apply <m>\vec{x}(0)=\begin{bmatrix}1 \\ 2\end{bmatrix}</m>.</p>

    <me>
      C_1\begin{bmatrix}0 \\ 1\end{bmatrix}
      +
      C_2\begin{bmatrix}2 \\ 0\end{bmatrix}
      =
      \begin{bmatrix}1 \\ 2\end{bmatrix}.
    </me>

    <p>
      From the second component:  
      <m>C_1 = 2.</m>
    </p>

    <p>
      From the first component:
    </p>

    <me>
      2C_2 = 1
      \quad\Rightarrow\quad
      C_2 = \tfrac12.
    </me>

    <p>
      Thus the specific solution is
    </p>

    <me>
      \vec{x}(t)
      =
      e^{3t}
      \begin{bmatrix}
      \cos(2t)-4\sin(2t) \\
      2\cos(2t)+\frac{1}{2}\sin(2t)
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>

  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="twodimaut-correct-complex-evals">
  <!-- <title>Classifying a Two-Dimensional Linear System and Sketching Its Phase Portrait</title> -->

  <statement>
    <p>
      Consider the autonomous system
    </p>

    <me>
      \vec{x}' =
      A\vec{x},
      \qquad
      A =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      1 \amp -3 \\
      1 \amp 1
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>Compute the eigenvalues of <m>A</m>.</li>
        <li>Classify the equilibrium at <m>\vec{x}=\vec{0}</m> (node, spiral, saddle, center).</li>
        <li>Determine whether the equilibrium is stable or unstable.</li>
        <li>Describe qualitatively the behavior of trajectories.</li>
      </ol>
    </p>

  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Compute <m>\det(A - \lambda I)</m> directly:
    </p>

    <me>
      \det(A-\lambda I)
      =
      \begin{vmatrix}
      1-\lambda \amp -3 \\
      1 \amp 1-\lambda
      \end{vmatrix}.
    </me>

    <p>
      Solve for the eigenvalues.  
      Use the <em>real part</em> of the eigenvalues to determine stability.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      The eigenvalues are
    </p>

    <me>
      \lambda = 1 \pm i\sqrt{3}.
    </me>

    <p>
      Since the real part is positive, the equilibrium is an
      <strong>unstable spiral source</strong>.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p><strong>(a) Compute eigenvalues.</strong></p>

    <me>
      \det(A-\lambda I)
      =
      (1-\lambda)(1-\lambda) - (-3)(1)
      =
      (1-\lambda)^2 + 3.
    </me>

    <p>Expand:</p>

    <me>
      (1-\lambda)^2 + 3
      =
      \lambda^2 - 2\lambda + 1 + 3
      =
      \lambda^2 - 2\lambda + 4.
    </me>

    <p>
      Solve:
    </p>

    <me>
      \lambda
      =
      \frac{2 \pm \sqrt{4 - 16}}{2}
      =
      1 \pm i\sqrt{3}.
    </me>

    <p><strong>(b) Classification.</strong></p>

    <p>
      The eigenvalues are complex (non-real), so the origin is a
      <em>spiral equilibrium</em>.
    </p>

    <p><strong>(c) Stability.</strong></p>

    <p>
      The real part of both eigenvalues is <m>1 &gt; 0</m>.
      Therefore solutions spiral <em>away</em> from the origin:
      the equilibrium is an <strong>unstable spiral</strong>.
    </p>

    <p><strong>(d) Qualitative behavior.</strong></p>

    <ul>
      <li>Solutions spiral outward as <m>t \to \infty</m>.</li>
      <li>The imaginary part <m>\sqrt{3}</m> causes oscillations.</li>
      <li>The positive real part produces exponential growth in radius.</li>
      <li>Rotation is counterclockwise because the off-diagonal entries produce that turning direction.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      The phase portrait in <xref ref="phaseportrait-spiralsource"/> shows several outward‑spiraling trajectories along with the direction field.
    </p>

     <p>
    <figure xml:id="phaseportrait-spiralsource">
      <caption>A phase portrait illustrating the dynamics of the system.</caption>
      <image width="60%">
      <description>
        A phase portrait showing an unstable counterclockwise spiral around the origin
        for the system x' = Ax. Several solution curves spiral
        outward from the origin and a direction field is drawn with small arrows.
      </description>
        <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.15]
  % 1. Use a clipping path so spirals don't bleed into your margins
  \clip (-2.6,-2.6) rectangle (2.6,2.6);

  % 2. Vector field (keeping your logic)
  \foreach \x in {-2,-1.5,...,2}
    \foreach \y in {-2,-1.5,...,2}{
      \pgfmathsetmacro{\vx}{\x - 3*\y}
      \pgfmathsetmacro{\vy}{\x + \y}
      \pgfmathsetmacro{\norm}{sqrt(\vx*\vx + \vy*\vy+0.1)}
      \draw[->, thin, gray!80] (\x,\y) -- ++(0.2*\vx/\norm, 0.2*\vy/\norm);
    }

  % 3. Fixed Spiral Trajectories 
  % We use a smaller domain (0:2.5) so exp(t) stays small
  \foreach \offset in {0, 1.2, 2.4, 3.6, 4.8}{
    \draw[blue, thick, smooth, domain=0:2.5, samples=100, variable=\t]
         plot ({0.2*exp(\t/1.5)*cos(deg(4*\t + \offset))},
               {0.2*exp(\t/1.5)*sin(deg(4*\t + \offset))});
  }

  % 4. Axes
  \draw[->, thick] (-2.5,0) -- (2.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
  \draw[->, thick] (0,-2.5) -- (0,2.5) node[above] {$y$};
  \fill (0,0) circle (2pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>
  </p>

  </solution>

</exercise>

  </exercises>

